//------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Version 1.12.0 "Puente de Santiago" - May 02, 2026

Recommended Versions:
- Make sure Catch 2 v3 is installed in your system
- Boost version 1.91 or higher is required
- CMake version 3.27 or higher is required
- GCC is recommended to be at least version 13, clang should be at least version 18.
  Earlier versions might work if C++20 is sufficiently supported
- We suggest to use Ubuntu 24.04 LTS or 26.04 LTS as the host platform if you plan
  to use the CUDA example.

********************** INCOMPATIBILITIES / PLEASE NOTE *************************

- THE INDIVIDUAL MODEL HAS CHANGED FUNDAMENTALLY: the former tree of parameter
  objects (GParameterSet holding GConstrainedDoubleObject / GDoubleCollection /
  GParameterObjectCollection nodes, each carrying its own adaptor) has been removed
  entirely and replaced by a single, FLAT genome. A problem is now defined by
  subclassing GFlatGenome (typically through the CRTP helper
  GFlatIndividualT<Derived>) and building a flat, fixed-structure array of
  double / float / int32 / bool values once in the constructor with GGenomeBuilder
  (addDoubleGroup, addDouble, addInt32Group, addBoolArray, ...) installed via
  setGenome(); values are read by position in fitnessCalculation() via
  streamline<T>() / streamlineFP(). The entire GParameterBase / GParameterT /
  parameter-object and adaptor-object hierarchy and the GParameterSet /
  GParameterTree / GTreeGenome classes are gone. This is SOURCE-breaking for every
  problem definition and changes the serialization format (older archives no longer
  load). See docs/writing-optimization-problems.md and examples/geneva/10_GStarter
  for the new authoring model.

- CONFIG-STRIP: mutation adaptors no longer live on the genome. The genome's shared
  layout (GGenomeLayout) is now STRUCTURE-ONLY (type / bounds / grouping / labels);
  the Gauss / bi-Gauss / flip mutation settings live on a separate, OPTIMIZATION-
  ALGORITHM-OWNED adaption configuration (GAdaptionConfig), authored at setup via a
  fluent API (oa::makeAdaptionConfig(genome) + cfg->groupDouble(i).gauss(...) /
  cfg->forLabel(...)) and distributed via an OA's setAdaptionConfig(),
  Go2::registerAdaptionConfig(personality, cfg) or oa::StandaloneAdapter(genome,
  cfg). The adaption logic itself moved off the individual into stateless free
  functions in geneva/oa/. An adapting algorithm (EA / SA) that is handed NO adaption
  config is now a HARD ERROR -- there is no automatic derivation from the genome.
  SOURCE-breaking for all problem code; serialization-affecting.

- The optimizable-entity / genome layer was renamed from the namespace
  Gem::Geneva::Parameters to Gem::Geneva::Genome (the pervasive short alias gpar::
  became gen::), and the value-array type formerly named Genome is now GenomeData.
  Several compound identifiers were renamed to match (GParameterSet* ->
  GOptimizableEntity*, parameterset_processing_result -> individual_processing_result).
  SOURCE-breaking for code referencing the old names; changes the
  BOOST_CLASS_EXPORT GUIDs (serialized class identities differ).

- Individual factories have been unified. Every hand-written GXxxIndividualFactory
  is replaced by the generic, config-driven
  Gem::Geneva::Genome::GFlatIndividualFactory<Derived>; a concrete individual now
  supplies static hooks (a nested Config struct, describeConfig(), buildGenome(),
  and optionally buildAdaptionConfig() / applyConfig() / finalize()) instead of a
  bespoke factory class. As part of this the legacy Gem::Common::GFactoryT
  scaffolding was simplified: the unused GOneTimeRefParameterT helper and the
  per-production id-sequence / write-mode machinery (GFACTTORYFIRSTID /
  GFACTORYWRITEID / the atomic id_ counter / getId()) were removed, and the
  protected getObject_() virtual lost its trailing id parameter. The
  optimization-algorithm factories were likewise unified behind a
  GOptimizationAlgorithmT CRTP scaffold. SOURCE-breaking for code that subclassed
  the individual or algorithm factories.

- The population is now a vector of GIndividualSlot objects (a problem individual
  plus its per-run, optimization-algorithm-owned scratch) rather than bare
  individuals. All per-individual OA state -- adaption sigmas, swarm velocity /
  personal best, conjugate-gradient history, and the algorithm personality traits --
  now lives on the slot's transient scratch, not on the (serialized) individual
  identity, and is preserved across a checkpoint resume. The individual is thereby
  reduced to pure problem data; this changes the checkpoint serialization format.

- ADDITIVE (opt-in) optimizer modes on the conjugate-gradient descent (beyond the
  GD-retirement / line-search / HESSE / MINOS items below): an L-BFGS quasi-Newton
  mode, configurable beta formulas (Fletcher-Reeves / Hestenes-Stiefel+ / Dai-Yuan
  in addition to Polak-Ribiere+), and optional O(h^2) central-difference gradients;
  plus Nelder-Mead outside-contraction and an opt-in oriented restart. Defaults are
  unchanged.

- ADDITIVE (opt-in): a new high-dimensional optimization algorithm,
  GSepCmaEvolutionStrategy (mnemonic "sepcma"). It is a from-scratch CSA evolution
  strategy with an optional separable (diagonal) covariance adaptation (sep-CMA-ES).
  Each generation it samples lambda offspring x = m + sigma * sqrt(C) o N(0,I) from a
  single search distribution it owns internally (mean m, global step sigma, diagonal
  covariance C, evolution paths p_sigma / p_c), evaluates them through the one
  process consumer, weighted-recombines the mu best and updates m, sigma (CSA) and C.
  All updates are O(n) per generation with dimension-scaled constants (Hansen 2016;
  Ros & Hansen 2008), so it scales to tens of thousands of parameters where the
  self-adaptive EA's per-individual sigma adaption stalls. It needs no adaption config
  (it carries no per-genome adaptors). A NSGA-II-style multi-objective selection mode
  (non-dominated sort + crowding distance) is available via setParetoMode(true).
  Use it via the "sepcma" mnemonic or by constructing GSepCmaEvolutionStrategy directly.

- boost::lexical_cast is no longer used anywhere in Geneva: enum <-> string
  conversion goes through the stream operators / Gem::Common::fromString<T>, and the
  one remaining (test-only) use was removed. Internal only -- not source-breaking.

- The per-parameter "name" concept has been removed (the parameter objects that
  carried it are themselves gone -- see the flat-genome note above): there is no
  per-parameter name, and all by-name parameter addressing is gone -- the former
  getParameterName() / setParameterName(), the std::map<std::string,
  std::vector<T>> "streamline" / "assignValueVectors" overloads, getVariableNames(),
  and the by-name modes of
  getVarVal(). Parameters are addressed strictly by position (index) now. The
  parameter-scan and GProgressPlotter variable specifications therefore use the
  positional form (e.g. "d(0, -10, 10, 100)"); a name token in a spec is now a
  free-form display label, not a parameter lookup key. This is SOURCE-breaking
  for code calling get/setParameterName or the
  by-name streamline API, and it changes the serialization format (the
  parameter_name_ field is gone, so older archives no longer load). As a side
  effect GParameterPropertyParser was reimplemented as a small hand-written
  tokenizer and no longer depends on Boost.Spirit. (The former per-parameter
  default name was a generated UUID -- an ostringstream per parameter at
  construction plus a heap-string copy on every clone -- pure overhead that is
  now gone.)

- The runtime formula-expression facility has been removed: the Boost.Spirit
  grammar GFormulaParserT (and its math_logic_error exception family) and the
  GParameterSetFormulaConstraint that was its sole consumer are gone. Constraints
  are now expressed exclusively as C++ constraint objects -- subclasses of
  GOptimizableEntityConstraint (e.g. GDoubleSumConstraint, GSphereConstraint,
  GDoubleSumGapConstraint) -- which are strictly more versatile than a formula
  string: they see every parameter type (not just doubles), are type-safe and can
  branch/loop arbitrarily. The GExternalEvaluatorIndividual "bounds/expression"
  config option (which built formula constraints at run time) is likewise removed;
  it was a latent feature exercised by no shipped example, test or evaluator.
  SOURCE/CONFIG-breaking only for code that referenced GFormulaParserT or
  GParameterSetFormulaConstraint, or that fed a "bounds" section to the external
  evaluator. This removes Geneva's single largest remaining Boost.Spirit user.

- Boost.Spirit / Phoenix / Fusion are no longer used anywhere in Geneva. The
  last remaining qi::phrase_parse call sites -- the comma-separated number / tuple
  list parsers in GCommonHelperFunctions (stringToUIntVec / stringToDoubleVec /
  stringToUIntTupleVec) and the "idle(<ms>)" command parser in GBaseClientT --
  were rewritten as small hand-written scanners over std::from_chars, and a
  vestigial Fusion include was dropped from GPlotDesigner. Behaviour (including
  whitespace tolerance and rejection of malformed input) is preserved. These
  libraries were header-only and never find_package COMPONENTS, so no CMake change
  is needed; the heavy Spirit/Phoenix/Fusion template machinery simply no longer
  enters Geneva's translation units (a compile-time win). This is internal only --
  not source-breaking.

- Individuals (the flat GFlatGenome) now travel by std::unique_ptr rather than
  std::shared_ptr along the hot path: the optimization-algorithm population
  container, the courtier work item and wire payload, and the transport
  get/put callbacks all carry sole ownership now. Only genuinely shared
  objects (the asynchronous network sessions/nodes that use
  enable_shared_from_this, the broker, GFactoryT factory products and the
  best-results archives) remain shared_ptr. This is SOURCE-breaking for code
  that constructed individuals as shared_ptr and handed them to the OA/courtier
  API: build the population with unique_ptr (e.g. push_back a clone_unique() or
  a std::make_unique), and note that GCommandContainerT's payload accessor was
  split into a borrowing get_payload() (const reference) and an ownership-
  transferring release_payload(). GCommonInterfaceT gained clone_unique() and a
  load(const std::unique_ptr&) overload to support this.
- The flat genome's shared structural layout (bounds / grouping / labels) is no
  longer copied into every work item on the network path. A genome's serialized
  form now begins with a marker that selects one of two encodings: a self-contained
  form (used for checkpoints / files and whenever no networked transport is active)
  that still carries the full layout by value, and a transport "send-once" form in
  which the layout is referenced by a content id -- the full layout is sent to a
  given client only the first time that id is seen on its connection, and by id
  alone thereafter. Reconnecting and late-joining clients are handled
  transparently (a fresh connection re-receives the layouts it needs). For a
  large structured genome whose layout is identical
  across the whole population this cuts the per-item wire size substantially.
  Relatedly, a processed work item is by default returned to the server in a
  lightweight "results-only" form on all three networked transports (websocket,
  Asio and MPI) -- the computed results (including all evaluations of a
  multi-criterion individual) travel, but the input parameters do not, since the
  server still holds the originally-submitted item and grafts them back on. A
  client that has MODIFIED the individual (e.g. a network-tiered client that runs
  its own optimization and returns the best individual it found) opts into a full
  return via GOptimizableEntity::setReturnFullIndividual(true). A results-only
  return that arrives LATE -- after its batch was reconciled, so the original it
  would graft from is gone -- is dropped (and counted) rather than buffered for
  getOldWorkItems(), as its parameters can no longer be reconstructed.
  These added markers change the genome serialization format, so archives written
  by earlier 1.12.0 development builds no longer load.
- The separate gradient-descent algorithm (GGradientDescent) has been removed:
  conjugate gradient descent is now the single gradient-based local optimizer,
  with steepest descent available as its beta == 0 mode (the new gradient_method
  option / GConjugateGradientDescent::setGradientMethod()). The "gd" mnemonic is
  RETAINED as a backward-compatible alias that produces a conjugate gradient
  descent forced into steepest-descent mode, so `-a gd` still works. SOURCE-
  breaking only for code that named the GGradientDescent class or its header
  directly (use GConjugateGradientDescent); the GGradientDescent_PersonalityTraits
  marker and the "gd" mnemonic are unchanged.
- Gradient descent and conjugate gradient descent are no longer fixed-step. They
  now take a proper line-searched step (backtracking Armijo sufficient-decrease,
  via the new reusable Gem::Geneva::OptimizationAlgorithms::GLineSearch helper)
  along a normalized finite-difference gradient, with Polak-Ribiere+ conjugacy
  and Powell + periodic restarts. The step length is chosen to guarantee
  decrease, so a starting point can no longer move uphill, and convergence is
  markedly better than the old fixed step. The line-search probes are evaluated
  through the same consumer the algorithm uses, so they run on the GPU when the
  GPU consumer is active. The "step_size" configuration option is now the INITIAL
  trial step (the line search refines it) rather than a fixed multiplier.
- ADDITIVE (opt-in, off by default): conjugate gradient descent can now report a
  MINUIT-style parameter-error estimate at the converged minimum, configured via
  the new "error_estimation" option (0 none, 1 diagonal/parabolic, 2 full
  Hessian -> covariance) and "error_definition" (the error-definition UP: 1 for
  chi^2, 0.5 for -logL). It is computed by the reusable
  Gem::Geneva::OptimizationAlgorithms::GHesseError helper -- a finite-difference
  Hessian inverted (Gauss-Jordan) to a covariance matrix, giving per-parameter
  one-sigma errors and a curvature condition number; the curvature probes are
  evaluated through the active consumer like the line search. This is an
  original, clean-room implementation of the published HESSE method (James &
  Roos, Comp. Phys. Comm. 10, 1975); it uses no MINUIT/Minuit2 code.

- Static linking support (BUILDSTATIC / GENEVA_STATIC / GENEVA_STATIC=TRUE) has
  been removed. Geneva now builds exclusively as shared libraries. Any build scripts
  that set BUILDSTATIC="1" or pass -DGENEVA_STATIC=TRUE must be updated to remove
  that setting. The four Geneva shared libraries (gemfony-common, gemfony-hap,
  gemfony-courtier, gemfony-geneva) are always built
  as SHARED; BUILD_SHARED_LIBS is always ON; GEM_DYNAMIC is always defined.
- gemfony_exception was renamed to geneva_exception (~128 files). Any user code that
  catches or explicitly names this exception type must be updated accordingly.
- The Boost.Test library linkage has been removed from the build-system, instead Geneva now
  uses Catch2 v3 which needs to be present on the system in order to compile the code.
- Windows and macOS support has been removed. Geneva now targets Linux exclusively.
  The G_API_COMMON, G_API_HAP, G_API_COURTIER, G_API_GENEVA, and G_API_INDIVIDUALS
  symbol-visibility macros have been removed from all 2266 call sites and from
  GGlobalDefines.hpp entirely (they were no-ops on Linux without -fvisibility=hidden).
  The GEM_*_EXPORTS preprocessor definitions and the GEM_DYNAMIC definition have been
  removed from the CMake build system. The PLATFORM_NEEDS_LIBRARY_LINKING variable
  always evaluates to FALSE and its conditional TARGET_LINK_LIBRARIES blocks have been
  removed from all five library CMakeLists.txt files. IdentifySystemParameters.cmake
  now supports only GCC >= 13 and Clang >= 18 on Linux.
- The optimization-algorithm and interface code has been reorganised into
  dedicated namespaces, and this is a SOURCE-BREAKING change for code that
  referenced these symbols directly. The optimization algorithms now live in
  Gem::Geneva::OptimizationAlgorithms (global alias `oa`) and the optimizer
  interfaces in Gem::Geneva::Interface (no short alias). The transitional
  using-declarations that briefly kept the old Gem::Geneva:: spelling working,
  and the old G_OptimizationAlgorithm_EvolutionaryAlgorithm.hpp forwarding
  header, have been removed -- there is no compatibility layer. The four genus
  classes were renamed (G_OptimizationAlgorithm_Base -> GBase,
  G_OptimizationAlgorithm_FactoryT -> GOAFactoryT,
  G_OptimizationAlgorithm_InitializerT -> GInitializerT,
  G_OptimizationAlgorithm_ParChild -> GParChild), the three interface classes
  were renamed (OptimizerIT -> GOptimizerIT, RateableI -> GRateableI,
  MutableI -> GMutableI), and FactoryStore -> GFactoryStore. The seven
  algorithm class names are UNCHANGED (GEvolutionaryAlgorithm,
  GSwarmAlgorithm, GSimulatedAnnealing, GGradientDescent,
  GConjugateGradientDescent, GNelderMead, GParameterScan) -- only their
  namespace and header location changed. Every header was renamed to its
  primary class and relocated: geneva/<X>.hpp -> geneva/oa/G<X>.hpp and the
  interface headers -> geneva/Interface/G<X>.hpp; #include paths must be updated
  accordingly. User code must qualify these types with oa:: / Interface:: (or the
  full namespace) and fix its include paths. The common high-level workflow
  (subclass GFlatGenome / GFlatIndividualT, drive it via Gem::Geneva::Go2 with a
  JSON config and algorithm mnemonics) is otherwise unaffected -- Go2 itself is unchanged
  -- except for the updated include paths. Note also that, as a consequence,
  Boost.Serialization class-export keys changed: checkpoint/archive files
  written by earlier Geneva versions are not loadable by this version (this
  archive break was a deliberate, accepted decision).
- Hap now uses the xoshiro256++ engine (64-bit, with a SIMD bulk-refill backend
  and an optional cuRAND GPU backend): the previously experimental Hap2 was promoted
  to be the one and only Hap. The legacy mt19937-based Hap and the Hap-vs-Hap2
  benchmark were removed. Namespace, library and include prefix are unchanged
  (Gem::Hap / gemfony-hap / <hap/...>), so consumers need no changes. Note that the
  RNG result_type is now 64-bit (uint64_t) instead of 32-bit; this is transparent to
  code that uses the proxy as a UniformRandomBitGenerator with <random> distributions
  (as Geneva does throughout), but code that consumed the raw 32-bit output directly
  would need review.
- The GBoundedBufferT bounded buffer was replaced by GBlockingMPMCQueueT
  (common/GBlockingMPMCQueueT.hpp), a modernised multi-producer/multi-consumer
  blocking queue: one perfect-forwarding push family, std::optional-returning pop
  (move-only friendly), if-constexpr bounded/unbounded handling, a close() shutdown
  that wakes all waiters, [[nodiscard]] throughout, and a fixed remaining_space()
  (the old unbounded case underflowed). The Hap, Hap2 and Courtier (GBufferPortT)
  buffers were migrated and GBoundedBufferT was removed. SOURCE-breaking for code
  that used Gem::Common::GBoundedBufferT directly.
- ADDITIVE (no behaviour change by default): GBlockingMPMCQueueT gained an optional,
  interface-identical sibling -- a preallocated ring-buffer queue
  (common/GPreallocatedMPMCQueueT.hpp) plus a thin compile-time facade
  Gem::Common::GMPMCQueueT<T, Cap, QueueBackend> that selects either backend. A shared
  concept (common/GQueueCommon.hpp, MPMCQueue) guarantees the two backends are
  interchangeable, and a type-parametrised conformance test exercises both. The
  preallocated backend never allocates per item and stores elements contiguously, but is
  bounded only (Cap > 0); the deque backend remains the default and the only one that
  supports the unbounded case. GRandomFactory's package buffers now go through the facade
  with the deque backend by default (behaviour unchanged); defining
  GENEVA_HAP_FACTORY_QUEUE_PREALLOCATED at configure time switches them to the ring buffer.
- GThreadPool (common/GThreadPool.hpp) was reimplemented on top of Geneva's own
  primitives -- a GThreadGroup of std::jthread workers draining a GBlockingMPMCQueueT
  task queue -- and no longer depends on Boost.ASIO (the io_context / work guard are
  gone). The public API is a drop-in match (construct with a thread count,
  async_schedule() returning a std::future, wait(), get/setNThreads), with two
  behavioural refinements: worker threads now start eagerly in the constructor, so
  getNThreads() reports the live worker count immediately instead of 0 until the first
  submission; and a thread count of 0 falls back to the hardware default (with a
  warning) rather than throwing on first submission. A fire-and-forget post() overload
  was added for tasks whose result need not be observed. The void/non-void
  async_schedule split was folded into a single if-constexpr template body.
- SOURCE/ABI-BREAKING: the Courtier library's submission machinery was redesigned around a
  span + policy model, and the entire legacy submission stack was removed. The broker
  (GBrokerT) now holds exactly ONE consumer; fanning work out to many workers/clients is the
  consumer's own responsibility (a thread pool locally, many clients over the network). A
  single thin executor (GExecutorT) hands a whole round -- a std::span over the population
  slice to be evaluated -- straight to the consumer, which evaluates and reconciles it in
  place against a GSubmissionPolicy (clone-on-partial-return for EA/SA/Swarm,
  full-success-or-fatal for GD/CGD/NM/ParameterScan, with a fail-on-no-return floor). The old
  per-executor resubmission logic, the raw/processed buffer-port queueing layer, the worker
  class and the base/local/networked-server consumer hierarchy were deleted. The transport
  infrastructure (network clients, sessions, MPI nodes, the GCommandContainerT wire protocol
  and MPI initialisation) was kept and relocated under courtier/transport/. Code that drove
  submission through the old Courtier API directly must be updated; the usual high-level path
  (subclass GFlatGenome, drive it via Go2 with a consumer mnemonic) is unaffected.
- The networked consumers (GAsioConsumerT, GWebsocketConsumerT and, when built,
  GMPIConsumerT) now share a common fan-in core (GNetworkedConsumerT): many optimization
  algorithms can submit to the SAME client pool concurrently, results are correlated by a
  (batch_id, slot) token, and stuck or lost work is recovered by an adaptive give-up window
  plus per-transport death detection (a time-based reclaim lease for asio/mpi; immediate
  session-death requeue for the websocket consumer). The consumer now waits INDEFINITELY for
  the first result of a run, so a server may be started before its clients arrive (e.g. under
  a batch-scheduling system, possibly hours later) without failing the run. The asio/beast/mpi
  transports were hardened against the "connections refused/dropped, or hangs, after a while"
  failure modes (full-duplex websocket client with an off-thread compute pool, per-session
  timeouts and back-off on the acceptors, bounded-with-grace MPI shutdown). Clients gained
  OPTIONAL work-item prefetch (off by default; --asio_prefetchDepth / --beast_prefetchDepth),
  which overlaps network transfer with computation while leaving the wire protocol unchanged.
  The processing-status enum value DO_IGNORE was renamed to UNPROCESSED.
- The geneva-individuals library has been dissolved into the geneva library.
  The reusable sample individuals now live in the Gem::Geneva::Individuals
  namespace (global alias `gind`), with headers in geneva/individuals/; the
  gemfony-geneva-individuals shared library no longer exists. SOURCE/ABI-
  breaking: qualify these types with gind:: (or the full namespace) and
  update #include paths accordingly.
- Following the same scheme, the unit-test sample individuals
  GTestIndividual1, GTestIndividual2 and GTestIndividual3 (with the
  PERFOBJECTTYPE helper) were moved out of the Gem::Tests namespace into
  Gem::Geneva::Individuals (alias `gind`); their headers moved from
  geneva/GTestIndividualN.hpp to geneva/individuals/GTestIndividualN.hpp.
  SOURCE-breaking for code that referenced these types or included those
  headers directly. As with the optimization-algorithm reorganisation,
  their Boost.Serialization class-export keys changed accordingly
  (these are test fixtures; the archive break is a deliberate, accepted
  decision).
- The Gem::Tests namespace has been removed entirely. Its remaining
  occupants were reorganised under Gem::Geneva: the StandardTests_*
  unit-test driver templates moved to Gem::Geneva::Tests, and the
  GOptimizationBenchmark configuration parser moved to
  Gem::Geneva::Benchmarks. Additionally, the benchmark-function library
  namespace Gem::Geneva::BM (in the installed header
  geneva/individuals/GBenchmarkFunctions.hpp) and the GCUDAOptBenchmark
  classes (previously in bare Gem::Geneva) were consolidated into
  Gem::Geneva::Benchmarks (global alias `gbm`), so Gem::Geneva::BM::*
  is now Gem::Geneva::Benchmarks::* / gbm::*. SOURCE-breaking for code
  that referenced Gem::Tests::*, Gem::Geneva::BM::*, or those benchmark
  classes directly.
- MINOR / low impact (SOURCE-breaking): the swap() member functions have been
  removed from the GContainerT hierarchy (GContainerT::swap and
  GParameterSet::swap). They were only ever used by unit tests; no production
  code relied on them. To exchange two parameter objects, swap the owning
  shared_ptr instead (e.g. std::swap(ptrA, ptrB)) -- same effect in O(1), and
  each object keeps its own (correct) processing state.
- CONFIG-FILE BREAKING: all configuration-file parameter names are now spelled
  in snake_case (e.g. "maxIteration" -> "max_iteration", "parDim" -> "par_dim",
  "adProb" -> "ad_prob", "nNeighborhoodMembers" -> "n_neighborhood_members").
  Previously the shipped JSON files used camelCase keys while the code registered
  snake_case keys, so every camelCase config value was silently ignored and the
  built-in defaults were used instead. The keys are now consistent, so config
  files are honoured again -- but any existing user config files with the old
  camelCase keys must be updated (easiest: delete them and let Geneva regenerate
  fresh snake_case defaults). A note: a long-standing typo in one key was fixed
  in the same pass ("indivdualUpdateStallCounterThreshold" ->
  "individual_update_stall_counter_threshold"). To catch such drift in future,
  GParserBuilder now warns about unknown configuration-file keys (escalatable to
  an error via GParserBuilder::setUnknownKeyIsError(true)).
- MINOR (SOURCE-breaking): several Go2 public-API changes. The redundant
  getClientMode() was removed -- use clientMode() instead (it returned exactly
  the same flag). The Go2 iteration-offset accessors (setOffset() /
  setIterationOffset() / getIterationOffset()) were removed entirely: the
  GOptimizerIT interface method optimize(offset) now actually honours its
  argument (previously it was ignored and the offset was taken from a separate
  member), so pass the desired start offset to optimize(offset) directly instead.
  User code calling the removed accessors must be updated. Internally,
  Go2 no longer special-cases the MPI consumer when deciding client/server mode:
  every consumer now reconciles the requested --client flag itself via the new
  virtual GBaseConsumerT::determineClientMode() (the MPI consumer derives its role
  from its process rank, as before) -- runtime behaviour is unchanged.
- SOURCE/ABI-BREAKING: the monolithic GObject base class has been removed.
  GObject was the single common root of the entire Geneva object model; it has
  been decomposed into seven independent per-category CRTP roots, each deriving
  from Gem::Common::GCommonInterfaceT<Root> (which carries the former
  clone()/clone<>()/compare()/name()/toString/fromString/file-IO/load() public
  interface): GParameterBase (parameter objects), GParameterSet (individuals),
  Gem::Geneva::OptimizationAlgorithms::GBase (algorithms), GAdaptorT<>
  (adaptors), GPersonalityTraits (personality traits), the constraint root
  GPreEvaluationValidityCheckT<> (validity checks), and GBasePluggableOM
  (pluggable optimization monitors). Any user code that referenced
  Gem::Geneva::GObject directly -- as a type, an explicit base class, a
  GObject* pointer/cast, or via a GObject::-qualified call -- must be updated to
  the relevant category root (most users never do: the standard workflow
  subclasses GFlatGenome and drives it via Go2, which is unaffected). The
  former static SIGHUP signal API moved off GObject into free functions in the
  new header geneva/GSigHupHandler.hpp: GObject::sigHupHandler ->
  Gem::Geneva::sigHupHandler and GObject::G_SIGHUP_SENT() ->
  Gem::Geneva::G_SIGHUP_SENT() (same signal, same semantics). As with the other
  1.12.0 reorganisations, Boost.Serialization base-object chains changed
  (base_object<GObject> is gone), so this is covered by the already-accepted
  archive break -- checkpoint/archive files from earlier versions are not
  loadable. The on-the-wire member layout of every concrete class is otherwise
  unchanged within 1.12.0.

************************* Noteworthy Changes ***********************************

- The evolutionary algorithm GEvolutionaryAlgorithm ("ea") gained a selectable
  step-size-control strategy on the OA-owned adaption config that improves
  convergence near the optimum at high dimension: SELF_ADAPT (the classic
  mutative sigma self-adaption), SELF_ADAPT_SCALED (the default -- the sigma
  self-adaption rate is scaled by the parameter count, tau = c/sqrt(2n), instead
  of the fixed rate, which is far too hot at large n), ONE_FIFTH (Rechenberg 1/5
  success rule on a single global sigma) and CSA (cumulative step-size adaptation);
  it also optionally intermediate-recombines the per-individual sigma. The former
  separate, non-adaptive EA has been folded in: it is exactly the SELF_ADAPT mode
  with recombine_sigma=false, so the legacy behaviour is reproduced bit-for-bit
  (up to the RNG seed) by selecting that. On a 1000-parameter sphere over a fixed
  budget the legacy SELF_ADAPT stalls at the starting fitness while the default
  scaled controller reaches less than half of it.

- A server binary now uses a single courtier consumer for everything, held in a
  process-global registry (GConsumerRegistry). Algorithms are transport-agnostic:
  they submit through that one consumer and get back a fully evaluated population,
  never knowing whether evaluation happened locally, on a GPU, or on a remote
  client. The consumer is established once -- by Go2 (from the command-line
  mnemonic / default), or manually via Go2::registerConsumer() -- and every
  algorithm reads it from the registry. Consequently the per-algorithm
  GOptimizationAlgorithmBase::setBroker() and setLocalConsumer() (and the
  local_consumer_kind enum) have been REMOVED, along with the per-algorithm
  execution-policy plumbing; a standalone algorithm with no consumer set lazily
  builds a default local thread-pool consumer on first use. Nested optimizations
  (the meta-EA, the post-optimizer) run their inner work off this consumer -- the
  meta-EA on its own orchestration pool, the post-optimizer inline -- so the one
  consumer is never re-entered.

- Meta-optimization (tuning an optimization algorithm's own parameters) now has a
  dedicated driver, GMetaEvolutionaryAlgorithm. It is a standard evolutionary
  algorithm whose population members are "umbrella" individuals, each running a
  sub-optimization; it evaluates those umbrella individuals on its own thread pool
  while their sub-optimizations submit their work to the normal work consumer. This
  keeps a single work consumer for the process and lets the sub-optimizations run in
  parallel without the meta level competing for that consumer. Example 11 uses it.

- The CUDA random-number backend no longer leaves its output buffer unfilled when
  a GPU call fails: a failed cuRAND/CUDA call (which under heavy concurrent demand
  can be a transient launch failure) previously returned with the buffer untouched,
  so stale/uninitialised memory could be handed out as random numbers. It now falls
  back to a CPU generator for that batch so the buffer is always filled with real
  randomness; the GPU failure is still reported, so the underlying problem stays
  visible.

- The evolutionary algorithm now publishes a tunable manifest
  (GEvolutionaryAlgorithm::tunableManifest()) describing the knobs a meta-optimizer
  may tune -- each with a name, value channel and default search range. The
  meta-optimizer individual builds its search genome from this manifest (one
  labelled group per knob, the label being the knob name) and reads the values back
  by name. This replaces the previous hand-numbered parameter enum and the index
  arithmetic over the two value channels, so a knob can be added or reordered
  without silently shifting another. The meta-optimizer's configuration file keys
  are unchanged.

- Networked late returns are handled more completely. The work-item correlation id
  is now 64-bit, with a process-wide, non-wrapping batch id, so a stale result can
  never be misrouted to the wrong submitter once several algorithms share one
  consumer. And a slow-but-alive worker's result that returns after its round closed
  is no longer necessarily lost: when the late-return buffer is enabled, the consumer
  retains a copy of each un-returned work item for a bounded number of rounds, so a
  late results-only return (which carries no input parameters) can still have them
  grafted back and be reaped, instead of dropped. Only a late result with no retained
  original left to graft from is dropped.

- Preliminary support for two new local optimization algorithms:
  Nelder-Mead (the downhill-simplex method; class GNelderMead,
  mnemonic "nm") and Conjugate Gradient Descent (Polak-Ribiere+;
  class GConjugateGradientDescent, mnemonic "cgd"). Both integrate
  with the usual Go2 / factory / personality-traits machinery and can
  be selected like any other algorithm via their mnemonic. These
  implementations are new and should be considered preliminary; their
  interfaces and defaults may still change.

- New GContainerT<T, StoragePolicy> class supersedes GPODVectorT and
  GPtrVectorT with a unified, policy-based design. GPodContainerT and
  GPtrContainerT aliases preserve the old call sites.

- GCanvas (the Mona-Lisa PPM canvas) moved out of the Common library
  into example 15 — it was application-specific, not a general-purpose
  utility.

- Clang build support added (>= 18); GCC remains supported (>= 13).
  Compiler selectable via the new COMPILER variable in
  genevaConfig.gcfg, resolved by prepareBuild.sh.

- prepareBuild.sh overhauled with new options (--clean, --build,
  --generate-preset, --dryrun). CMakePresets.json added for IDE
  integration.

- CUDA improvements: GPU-based random numbers in Hap (USECUDARNG),
  a new CUDA optimisation example, and a benchmark. Toolkit pinning
  via CUDA_NVCC / CUDA_ROOT in genevaConfig.gcfg.

- Two rounds of Common-library code-review hardening (2026-05-14
  and 2026-05-15 reviews): 4 HIGH, 12 MEDIUM and 10 LOW findings
  closed. Highlights: concurrency races fixed in GSingletonT,
  GLogger, GFactoryT and GThreadGroup; iterator-invalidation and
  silent-insert bugs in GContainerT and GGlobalOptionsT; numeric
  edge cases in narrow_cast; formula-parser stack bounds-checking;
  regex-free placeholder replacement; gLineStyle enum-value
  collision fixed. See the commit messages for full detail.

- Wide-ranging C++20 modernisation: C++20 concepts in type traits;
  std::numbers, std::scoped_lock, std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<T>>,
  range-for with structured bindings; std::variant / std::any /
  std::optional / std::filesystem replacing their Boost counterparts
  where Geneva used them; deprecated boost/spirit/include/phoenix_*.hpp
  paths updated.

- Significant Catch2 v3 unit-test coverage added or extended across
  Common, Hap, Courtier and Geneva.

- Fixed a long-standing bug in GTupleIO: g_to_string and the tuple
  operator<< rendered multi-element tuples with the first element
  repeated in place of later ones; fitness logs in the optimisation
  algorithms now show both components correctly.

- Fixed a bug in GThreadPool::async_schedule (void overload): the
  returned future was never satisfied on success, so any caller that
  blocked on .get() / .wait() saw a "Broken promise" failure.

- Fixed a use-after-end-iterator bug in GParserBuilder: writing a
  vector/array configuration file before any parsing had populated
  the parsed-value vector dereferenced an end iterator, producing
  silent garbage in the generated config (or a segfault, depending
  on allocator layout).

- Housekeeping: Gemfony branding removed from file headers; GBarrier
  removed; GGlobalDefines.hpp no longer generated from a CMake template;
  obsolete Boost version guards dropped; five BOOST_ASSERT(0) sites in
  the formula parser replaced with assert(false).

- Internal: the wire (de)serialization of the networked session protocol was
  de-duplicated into a shared GWireCodec (courtier). The three transports
  (Asio, websocket, MPI) previously each open-coded the GWireSerializationScope
  wrap around a command container and the REQUEST_LAYOUT / SEND_LAYOUT layout
  cache-miss messages (with the subtle null-scope discipline); they now call
  wireEncode / wireDecode / buildLayoutRequest / parseLayoutReply / buildLayoutReply.
  Behaviour-preserving (the on-wire bytes are unchanged). The server-side request
  dispatch was likewise de-duplicated into a shared GServerSessionLogic: a pure
  serveWorkItem() (check a work item out -> COMPUTE / NODATA), used by all three
  transports, and a synchronous handleServerRequest() (GETDATA / RESULT / REQUEST_LAYOUT
  -> response bytes) shared by the request/response transports (Asio, websocket); the
  MPI server keeps its own split receive/send structure and reuses serveWorkItem().

- Fixed: the MPI consumer ignored its command-line options. The mpi_asyncReq /
  mpi_nHandlerThreads / mpi_cleanSessInterval / mpi_serializationMode options were
  registered (accepted) but never read back or forwarded -- the consumer always used
  its built-in defaults. They are now threaded through ConsumerSpec into the
  MPIConsumerConfig (mpi_nHandlerThreads == 0 still means hardware concurrency; the
  mpi_cleanSessInterval default was aligned to the consumer's own 1000 ms so the
  default behaviour is unchanged).

- Fixed: the MPI consumer no longer has a fixed 20 KB message-size cap. Receives previously posted a
  fixed-size buffer (GMPICONSUMERMAXMESSAGESIZE = 20 KB); a large genome's first (full-layout) work
  item or a big SEND_LAYOUT reply that exceeded it could not be delivered. The receivers (the master's
  single listener thread and the worker's single IO loop) now MPI_Iprobe + MPI_Get_count to learn each
  message's exact size and receive it into an exactly-sized buffer, so messages of any size are handled.
  The now-obsolete cap constant and the send-side size checks were removed. (Handler threads only send,
  so the probe-then-receive on the single-threaded receive paths is race-free.)

- GEvolutionaryAlgorithmPostOptimizer (the --usePostProcessor refiner) lost its vestigial
  executor_config_file_ member (and the matching constructor parameter, set/getExecutorConfigFile
  accessors and serialized field): the executor model was removed in the single-consumer rework and
  raw_processing_ evaluates the inner EA inline, so the executor config was stored and serialized but
  never used. Its serialize() also no longer carries a stale "how to initialise the EA factory" TODO --
  the factory is rebuilt from the (serialized) oa_config_file_ on each run, so a resumed post-optimizer
  reconstructs it identically. SOURCE-breaking for the 3-argument constructor; changes the
  post-optimizer's serialized form.

- The MPI helper utilities in courtier/GMPIHelperFunctions (the MPIStatusCode / MPICompletionStatus
  types and the mpi*() functions) were moved out of the global namespace into Gem::Courtier, removing
  the global unscoped enumerators SUCCESS / STOPPED / ERROR (a portability hazard against headers that
  define those as macros). SOURCE-breaking only for code that referenced these helpers unqualified.

- Fixed: GEvolutionaryAlgorithm::populationSanityChecks_ now validates the population size for the
  PARETO sorting modes too. Previously MUPLUSNU_PARETO / MUCOMMANU_PARETO bypassed the check, so a
  too-small population (fewer children than parents in comma selection, or no children in plus
  selection) was accepted and only misbehaved later; they now carry the same requirements as their
  single-evaluation counterparts (MUCOMMANU_PARETO >= 2*n_parents, MUPLUSNU_PARETO > n_parents).

- Robustness: GSwarmAlgorithm::adjustNeighborhoods now guards a completely empty first-iteration
  population in all builds (previously a DEBUG-only check; in a release build a total first-iteration
  work-item loss reached this->front() on an empty container -- undefined behaviour). It now throws a
  clean exception. Two long-standing TODOs there were resolved: the empty/partial-neighborhood backfill
  (including an entirely empty neighborhood, e.g. after networked timeouts) was confirmed correct and
  documented; and getFirstNIPosVec() gained the missing (DEBUG) neighborhood-size-vector bounds check.

- The bi-gaussian adaptor configuration (GAdaptionConfig::biGauss) gained the optional
  min_ad_prob / max_ad_prob clamp parameters (default [0, 1]) that the single-gaussian
  gauss() already exposed; previously biGauss hardcoded the [0, 1] ad_prob clamp with
  no way to override it. Additive -- existing biGauss() calls are unchanged.

- Single source of truth for each polymorphic class's local data members: a
  new localMembers() declaration (a tuple of named member references) from which
  load_(), compare_() and -- for the symmetric-serialization classes validated
  by the round-trip tests -- serialize() are derived, instead of maintaining the
  same member list in several parallel hand-written functions. Helpers added in
  GExpectationChecksT.hpp (make_member, g_load_members, g_compare_members,
  serialize_members). The remaining hand-listed classes were migrated to this
  pattern (GFixedSizePriorityQueueT, GBasePlotter, GPlotDesigner and the
  GStarterIndividual / GImagePOM / GImageIndividual example types). Two
  pre-existing serialize() omissions were fixed as part of this: GPlotDesigner
  did not serialize its plot list (plotters_cnt_), so a checkpointed monitor's
  accumulated plots were lost on resume; and GImageIndividual did not serialize
  its image dimensions or background / alpha mutation flags, so a networked
  worker or a resumed checkpoint silently reverted them to defaults. Both are
  now on the wire. (GImageIndividual's adaption_config_ remains deliberately
  transient -- a shared pointer to the optimization-algorithm-owned adaption
  configuration, neither serialized nor compared.)

- The Geneva standard unit tests (clone/modify/compare and TEXT/XML/BINARY
  (de)serialization round-trips) now also cover all seven optimization
  algorithms, the pluggable optimization monitors, and more individuals,
  validating the above across those types.

- Fixed a clone/load bug in copyCloneableSmartPointer(): when a polymorphic
  smart-pointer container slot held a different dynamic type than its source it
  called load() across incompatible types and threw a g_ptr_conversion error;
  it now deep-clones on a type mismatch and only load()s matching types.

- Fixed XML (de)serialization for GParameterSetConstraint, the multi-constraint
  classes, the parameter-scan parameter types and several factory bases: their
  serialize() used BOOST_SERIALIZATION_BASE_OBJECT_NVP on a templated base
  class, producing an XML element name containing '<'/'>' and throwing
  "Invalid XML tag name". TEXT and BINARY archives were unaffected.

- Fixed copy constructors that silently dropped state: GNAdpationsLogger and
  GAdaptorPropertyLoggerT omitted their oa::GBasePluggableOM base initializer,
  and GMetaOptimizerIndividualT dereferenced a null individual factory when
  copying a default-constructed instance.

- Fixed GDoubleSumGapConstraint::compare_, which compared only c_ although
  load_ copies both c_ and gap_.

- oa::GBase now re-exposes the inherited public load(const&), which had been
  name-hidden by the private split-serialization load(Archive&, unsigned int).

